<!--
  Linker roots of the browser build (WP-UI-09.T1). Read by the IL trimmer through TrimmerRootDescriptor in
  Spotledger.Client.Browser.csproj. Everything not named here and not reachable from the entry point is removed.

  Why these assemblies are rooted (SPK-07 REPORT, risk 1):
  The theme and control resources are loaded by URI at run time:
      SpotledgerTheme.MergeTokenResources    -> new ResourceInclude("avares://Spotledger.Client.Theme/Tokens.axaml")
      BrowserApp.Initialize                  -> new ResourceInclude("avares://Spotledger.Client.Controls/Themes/Controls.axaml")
  The trimmer cannot see a URI, so it removes the compiled XAML types behind it and the app stops at start-up
  with "No precompiled XAML found for avares://Spotledger.Client.Theme/Tokens.axaml". Rooting the assemblies that
  own compiled XAML keeps those types. The cost is size (the whole assemblies stay).

  How to remove a root: make the library load its resources without a URI (a typed style or resource class
  the code references), then delete the root and check the published size in assets.json. Those libraries
  are outside WP-UI-09.T1; the PR lists the change under Open questions.

  Not rooted on purpose: Spotledger.Client.Api (its System.Text.Json reflection use is an open trim warning,
  not a missing-type crash) and the Rust calculation module (it is a separate .wasm, not an assembly).
-->
<linker>
  <!-- Owns Tokens.axaml and the token resource dictionary. -->
  <assembly fullname="Spotledger.Client.Theme" preserve="all" />
  <!-- Owns Themes/Controls.axaml: the field controls of a document form. -->
  <assembly fullname="Spotledger.Client.Controls" preserve="all" />
  <!-- Owns the SpotGrid control theme, applied through the controls' styles. -->
  <assembly fullname="Spotledger.Client.Grid" preserve="all" />
  <!-- Owns ServerPickerView, LoginView and ShellView: Avalonia creates them from compiled XAML. -->
  <assembly fullname="Spotledger.Client.Shell" preserve="all" />
</linker>
